  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 228 PM CDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\nREST OF TODAY/TONIGHT: High pressure continues this afternoon and \nevening leading to an absolutely gorgeous early October day. Skies \nhave remained mostly clear with a few high clouds along the MO \nValley. Light and variable winds with temperatures warming into the \n60s and lower 70s. Continued the trend of the previous shifts to \nraise temperatures and lower dew points from the NBM given recent \ncool/wet bias.\n\nHigh pressure continues to slide east this evening and overnight, \nwhich allows southerly flow to return the area, especially west of I-\n29. Slightly adjusted temperatures as we may stay a bit warmer in \nthis area. Winds increase overnight as the SPG compresses between \ndeparting high pressure and the incoming front Monday. Lows tonight \nfall into the 40s and 50s, cooler in the east with clear skies and \nlight winds while warmer lows expected in the west with the quicker \nreturn of southerly flow.\n\nMONDAY: Based on the recent bias in the blend, raised temperatures \nand lowered dew points a touch for the afternoon/evening hours. Some \nlocations across south central SD should warm near 80 degrees, \nespecially as southerly flow and WAA increase. Winds strengthen as \nwell, with gusts reaching 40 mph in south central SD with 25-35 mph \ngusts common elsewhere. Will have to watch for stronger gusts in the \nhigher elevations overnight into Tuesday morning with the LLJ. Highs \nin the 70s for most (outside of south central SD). Lows Monday night \nremain in the 50s.\n\nTUESDAY-WEDNESDAY: Mid level wave and surface trough slide through \nTuesday morning. Expect dry conditions with a lack of appreciable \nmoisture. Northwesterly flow aloft and Tuesday at the surface may \nkeep temperatures a couple of degrees cooler on Tuesday, but still \nabove average. Stronger push of CAA with another mid level wave \nthrough the northwesterly flow cools temperatures slightly again on \nWednesday. Highs in the 70s to near 80 (Tuesday) and 70s (Wednesday) \nwith lows in the 40s and 50s. \n\nTHURSDAY-FRIDAY: Northwesterly flow remains over the central US as \nridging slowly breaks down over the western US by the end of the \nweek. A couple of waves move through during this time, bringing some \nlow end precipitation chances. Deterministic and ensemble guidance \nhas backed off a bit on the potential for precipitation Thursday \nwith less certainty in the pattern and location of any elevated \nfronts. Given the uncertainty, left the NBM for now. \n\nAs for Friday, guidance is in a bit better agreement with a surface \nwarm front lifting north and a more defined 700 mb wave as flow \naloft shifts westerly. Lower level moisture is better Thursday night \ninto Friday than Thursday morning/afternoon with more southeasterly \nflow. However, ensemble probability for more than 0.01\" of rain in a \n24 hour period remains low, at 25% or less. Confidence is low given \nquestions about forcing. Quite a bit of uncertainty in temperatures \nas well, with a 10 or more degree spread between the deterministic \nECMWF (warmer) and the GFS (cooler). Breezy conditions return Friday.\n\nNEXT WEEKEND: Pattern shift continues into next weekend with split \nmid and upper level flow. Expect warmer temperatures with \nsouthwesterly flow. A breezy to gusty weekend is expected, with the \nstrongest winds on Saturday. Guidance is quite varied on when/where \nany precipitation chances would be, with the GFS quickest with QPF. \nEnsemble probabilities of 24 hour precipitation of 0.10\" are low (as \nhigh as 30%) into early Monday morning.",
